#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
nyxus-undo — undo the LAST TRANSACTION, not the last hour.

★ WHY THIS EXISTS, and the one distinction it is built around.

  This build already ships the sledgehammer. snapper is configured on the
  btrfs install (root + home, NUMBER_LIMIT=10, timeline + cleanup timers),
  snap-pac takes a pre/post snapshot pair around EVERY pacman transaction,
  grub-btrfsd offers bootable snapshots, and Settings ▸ Backup lists the
  restore points with a per-row "Roll back". That is a rollback: it throws
  the whole system back to a moment in time and DISCARDS EVERYTHING AFTER
  IT — the document you wrote at lunch included.

  Nobody actually wants that. What a person wants is:

      "that last update broke my sound — undo THAT, and leave
       everything else I did today alone."

  `snapper undochange <pre>..<post>` is exactly that, and it is not a
  rollback: it reverts the file differences of ONE transaction and touches
  nothing else, so work done after the update survives. This file is the
  precise tool. Backup keeps the sledgehammer; the two sit side by side and
  say plainly which is which.

★ THE MECHANISM, VERIFIED AGAINST THE SHIPPED SNAPPER — not recalled.

  Measured 2026-08-16 against snapper 0.13.1-2 / libsnapper 8.0.0, the
  version in `packages.x86_64`, extracted from the pacman cache and run:

    · `snapper undochange <n1>..<n2> [files]` exists, and its summary line
      is `create:%d modify:%d delete:%d` ("nothing to do" when the undo
      statistic is empty, "Invalid snapshots." when a number is bad).
    · `snapper status <n1>..<n2>` prints one line per differing path: a
      six-character status string then the filename. Column 1 is `+`
      created, `-` deleted, `c` content changed, `t` type changed, `.` no
      change. That IS the file count this page promises.
    · `snapper --csvout list --columns <names>` is real, and the column
      vocabulary was read out of the binary's own error message:
      config, subvolume, number, default, active, type, date, user,
      used-space, cleanup, description, userdata, pre-number, post-number,
      post-date, read-only.
    · CSV output is a header row then one row per record — measured by
      actually running `--csvout list-configs` against a scratch root.

  So this file NEVER parses snapper's human table by column position. The
  Backup page does (`parts[5]`, `parts[6]`), and that parse silently shifts
  the day btrfs quota is enabled and snapper grows a "Used Space" column.
  Here every column is named on the command line and read back by header.

★ THE SAFETY ORDER, which is the whole argument and is pinned by gate 13q40.

  In `cmd_apply`, in this order, with nothing allowed to move ahead of it:

    1. CONSENT. No `--confirm`, no undo. Returns before anything is read.
    2. THE PLAN IS REBUILT FROM SCRATCH. The preview the person saw is not
       trusted; every guard runs again against the system as it is NOW.
    3. THE KERNEL GUARD. Ahead of the action, never beside it.
    4. THE PRE-UNDO SNAPSHOT. Undo is itself a change, so the undo can be
       undone. If the snapshot fails, NOTHING IS REVERTED — a revert with
       no way back is the failure mode this whole file exists to avoid.
    5. Only now, `snapper undochange`.
    6. The initramfs regeneration, if this was a kernel transaction.

★ THE KERNEL EDGE, stated because this build has already died of it once.

  `nyxus-postinstall` step 3 carries the recorded lesson: a mkinitcpio fix
  that existed on one install path and not the other shipped a machine that
  DIED AT ITS FIRST KERNEL UPDATE. Undoing a kernel transaction is the same
  hazard from the other side — put the old modules back under the new
  kernel image and the machine does not boot.

  Two things make it safe here, and both are CHECKED AT RUNTIME rather than
  assumed, because only one install path is ours:

    · /boot must be inside the snapshot. The Calamares layout puts the ESP
      at /boot/efi and gives /boot no subvolume of its own, so /boot rides
      `@` and vmlinuz + initramfs + /usr/lib/modules all come back TOGETHER.
      The TUI/archinstall path lays out whatever the user chose, so this
      file compares the SOURCE and FSROOT of /boot against those of /, and
      REFUSES with the reason if /boot is outside the root snapshot.
    · mkinitcpio must exist, and the initramfs is regenerated after the
      revert. Absent, this file REFUSES rather than half-working.

  A kernel undo is therefore either HANDLED (both conditions met, initramfs
  regenerated) or REFUSED WITH THE REASON. There is no third path.

⛔ NO TERMINAL, EVER — the owner's absolute rule (audit item 14). Everything
   here is reached from a Settings page through pkexec, and speaks `--json`.

⛔ WHAT THIS FILE WILL NOT DO
   · It will not pick a transaction for you when there is no honest answer.
     No pre/post pair, a pair whose "before" half has been cleaned up, a
     last transaction that was never snapshotted at all — each is its own
     refusal with its own exit code and its own sentence, never a spinner
     that ends in nothing and never a fake success.
   · It will not undo a transaction it cannot describe. The preview is
     computed by the same function the apply re-runs; a person cannot be
     shown one thing and given another.
   · It will not delete a snapshot. Ever. There is no delete in this file.

SURFACES
    nyxus-undo check [--json]     the cheap, unprivileged capability report
    nyxus-undo preview [--json]   what the last transaction was, and what
                                  undoing it would change
    nyxus-undo apply --confirm [--json]
                                  do it. Refuses without --confirm.
    --pair PRE..POST (preview/apply)
                                  an explicit snapshot pair instead of the
                                  newest snap-pac one. This is how
                                  nyxus-update --rollback (CS-1053) reverts
                                  the updater's own pre-update-/post-update-
                                  pair through THIS engine rather than
                                  growing a second undochange path.

EXIT CODES — a page reads --json, but a shell and a log see only this
    0   the command did what it said
    2   usage
    10  root is not btrfs
    11  snapper is not installed
    12  snapper has no configuration
    13  no snap-pac pre/post pair exists to undo
    14  the last transaction predates the snapshots that survive
    15  a kernel transaction, and /boot is OUTSIDE the root snapshot
    16  a kernel transaction, and mkinitcpio is missing
    17  apply was called without --confirm
    18  the pre-undo snapshot could not be taken — NOTHING was reverted
    19  snapper undochange itself failed
    20  the revert succeeded but the initramfs regeneration did not
    21  a --pair named a snapshot pair that does not exist

TEST HOOKS, and why they are safe
    NYXUS_UNDO_SNAPPER      path to the snapper binary
    NYXUS_UNDO_PACMAN_LOG   path to the pacman log
    NYXUS_UNDO_FINDMNT      path to findmnt
    NYXUS_UNDO_MKINITCPIO   path to mkinitcpio
    NYXUS_UNDO_CONFIG_DIR   the snapper configs directory `check` looks in
  They exist so the proof harness can run the REAL engine against a stubbed
  snapper with fabricated pre/post pairs — the confined-input pattern
  nyxus-hemera uses with NYXUS_HEMERA_HOME. They cannot be used to fool the
  privileged path: pkexec(1) documents that it replaces the environment with
  "a minimal known and safe environment", so nothing named here survives the
  pkexec boundary the Settings pages call through.

© 2026 JOSEPH A. SIERENGOWSKI · NYX-J5W-2026-SIERENGOWSKI-LOCKED
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import csv
import io
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
from datetime import datetime

APP_NAME = "Undo"

# ── exit codes, as named constants ───────────────────────────────────────
# They are module-level and named because gate 13q40 asserts they are all
# DISTINCT and all REACHED. A refusal that shares a number with another
# refusal is a refusal a caller cannot tell apart, which is how "it just
# didn't work" gets reported instead of the reason.
EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_USAGE = 2
EXIT_NOT_BTRFS = 10
EXIT_NO_SNAPPER = 11
EXIT_NO_CONFIG = 12
EXIT_NO_PAIR = 13
EXIT_PREDATES = 14
EXIT_KERNEL_BOOT_SPLIT = 15
EXIT_KERNEL_NO_MKINITCPIO = 16
EXIT_NO_CONSENT = 17
EXIT_SNAPSHOT_FAILED = 18
EXIT_UNDO_FAILED = 19
EXIT_INITRAMFS_FAILED = 20
EXIT_PAIR_MISSING = 21

# ── the refusals, in the words the page shows ────────────────────────────
# Each key is a real code path below, each carries its own exit code, and
# each says what is wrong AND what would make it work. "Unavailable" is not
# an answer; it is the absence of one.
REFUSALS = {
    "not-btrfs": (
        EXIT_NOT_BTRFS,
        "Undo needs the btrfs install — this system doesn't have it.",
        "Undoing one update means comparing two snapshots of the same "
        "filesystem, and only the btrfs layout Suxyn installs by default "
        "takes them. Nothing here can be undone precisely; a full "
        "reinstall of the package is the only way back."),
    "no-snapper": (
        EXIT_NO_SNAPPER,
        "snapper isn't installed.",
        "snapper is what records the before-and-after of every update. "
        "Install it from Backup and the next update will be undoable — "
        "this one already isn't."),
    "no-config": (
        EXIT_NO_CONFIG,
        "snapper is installed but has no configuration yet.",
        "Nothing is being snapshotted, so there is no 'before' to go back "
        "to. Backup can set this up; updates from then on become undoable."),
    "no-pair": (
        EXIT_NO_PAIR,
        "No update has been snapshotted yet — there is nothing to undo.",
        "snap-pac takes a pair of snapshots around every package "
        "transaction. No pair exists on this system, so the last update "
        "left no before-and-after to compare."),
    "predates": (
        EXIT_PREDATES,
        "The last update's 'before' snapshot is already gone.",
        "snapper keeps a limited number of restore points and the older "
        "half of that transaction has been cleaned up, so there is nothing "
        "left to compare it against. A full restore point from Backup is "
        "the remaining way back."),
    "kernel-boot-split": (
        EXIT_KERNEL_BOOT_SPLIT,
        "This was a kernel update, and undoing it here would leave the "
        "machine unable to boot.",
        "/boot is on a separate filesystem from the snapshot, so undoing "
        "would restore the old kernel modules while the NEW kernel image "
        "stayed in place — the exact mismatch that has already killed one "
        "machine on this build. Refused rather than half-done."),
    "kernel-no-mkinitcpio": (
        EXIT_KERNEL_NO_MKINITCPIO,
        "This was a kernel update, and the tool that rebuilds the startup "
        "image is missing.",
        "Putting an old kernel back without regenerating the initramfs can "
        "leave the machine unbootable, and mkinitcpio is not on this "
        "system to regenerate it. Refused rather than half-done."),
    "pair-missing": (
        EXIT_PAIR_MISSING,
        "The named snapshot pair does not exist.",
        "A caller asked for a specific PRE..POST pair (--pair) and at least "
        "one of the two numbers is not a snapshot on this system, or they "
        "are not in order. nyxus-update --rollback names the pair the "
        "updater itself took; if snapper has since cleaned one half up, "
        "Settings > System > Backup lists what remains."),
    "no-consent": (
        EXIT_NO_CONSENT,
        "Nothing was undone — this was not confirmed.",
        "An undo only ever runs when the person asked for it in as many "
        "words."),
}

# ── what makes a transaction a KERNEL transaction ────────────────────────
# Two independent signals, and the union is used, because either one alone
# has a blind spot: a package name can be a kernel package that happened to
# change nothing in /boot, and a path can be touched by a package whose name
# says nothing about kernels (a DKMS module rebuild).
#
# The PATH signal is the authority — it is what actually changed on disk.
KERNEL_PATH_PREFIXES = (
    "/boot/",
    "/usr/lib/modules/",
    "/usr/lib/initcpio/",
    "/usr/lib/firmware/",
    "/etc/mkinitcpio.d/",
)
KERNEL_PATH_EXACT = (
    "/etc/mkinitcpio.conf",
    "/etc/vconsole.conf",
)
# The NAME signal, as regexes so a linux-6.19 flavour nobody has named yet
# is still caught. `systemd` is here because it owns initcpio hooks; the
# ucode packages because they are early-boot images loaded before the
# initramfs; dkms because a rebuilt out-of-tree module is a kernel change
# whatever the package is called.
KERNEL_PKG_RE = re.compile(
    r"^(linux(|-\w+)(|-headers|-docs)|"
    r"linux-firmware(|-\w+)|"
    r"nvidia(|-.*)|"
    r"mkinitcpio(|-.*)|"
    r"(|.*-)dkms|"
    r"broadcom-wl(|-dkms)|"
    r"intel-ucode|amd-ucode|"
    r"systemd|systemd-libs|"
    r"booster|dracut)$")

# The pacman log's own grammar. The Updates page (`UpdatesPage._render_history`
# in nyxus_settings_pages_system.py) reads the same file with the same shape —
# `^\[timestamp\] ... upgraded name (versions)` — and this is deliberately the
# only OTHER place in the build that parses it, rather than a third copy
# inside the page for the undo feature. One parser per process: the page
# renders what this engine reports and never re-derives it.
PACMAN_LINE_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\[(?P<when>[^\]]+)\]\s+\[ALPM\]\s+"
    r"(?P<verb>installed|upgraded|removed|downgraded|reinstalled)\s+"
    r"(?P<pkg>\S+)\s+\((?P<vers>[^)]*)\)")

# Snapper's list columns, named on the command line so nothing is read by
# position. Verified against the shipped binary's own vocabulary.
LIST_COLUMNS = "number,type,pre-number,date,description,cleanup,userdata"


class UndoRefused(RuntimeError):
    """Raised when a guard says no. Carries the refusal key, nothing else.

    Every raise of this ends with the system exactly as the person left it.
    That is the entire point of the class.
    """

    def __init__(self, key: str) -> None:
        super().__init__(key)
        self.key = key


# ── the tools, and the test hooks that relocate them ─────────────────────

def _tool(env_name: str, default: str) -> str:
    override = os.environ.get(env_name)
    if override:
        return override
    found = shutil.which(default)
    return found or default


def snapper_bin() -> str:
    return _tool("NYXUS_UNDO_SNAPPER", "snapper")


def findmnt_bin() -> str:
    return _tool("NYXUS_UNDO_FINDMNT", "findmnt")


def mkinitcpio_bin() -> str:
    return _tool("NYXUS_UNDO_MKINITCPIO", "mkinitcpio")


def pacman_log_path() -> str:
    return os.environ.get("NYXUS_UNDO_PACMAN_LOG", "/var/log/pacman.log")


def config_dir_path() -> str:
    return os.environ.get("NYXUS_UNDO_CONFIG_DIR", "/etc/snapper/configs")


def run(argv: list, timeout: int = 120) -> tuple:
    """(rc, stdout, stderr). Never raises on a missing binary."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout)
        return p.returncode, p.stdout, p.stderr
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return 127, "", "%s: not found" % (argv[0] if argv else "?")
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "", "timed out after %ds" % timeout
    except Exception as e:                      # pragma: no cover
        return 1, "", str(e)


# ── the cheap reads ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def mount_identity(path: str) -> tuple:
    """(source, fsroot, fstype) of whatever filesystem `path` lives on.

    FSROOT is the subvolume path inside the btrfs device (`/@`, `/@home`).
    Comparing it is how "/boot is inside the root snapshot" is decided —
    same device AND a subvolume at or under root's — rather than guessing
    from the installer's layout, which is only ours on one of two paths.
    """
    rc, out, _ = run([findmnt_bin(), "-no", "SOURCE,FSROOT,FSTYPE",
                      "--target", path], timeout=8)
    if rc != 0 or not out.strip():
        return ("", "", "")
    parts = out.strip().splitlines()[0].split()
    while len(parts) < 3:
        parts.append("")
    # A btrfs SOURCE reads `/dev/sda2[/@]`; the bracket is the subvolume and
    # FSROOT repeats it, so the device half is what we keep.
    return (parts[0].split("[")[0], parts[1], parts[2])


def boot_inside_root_snapshot() -> bool:
    """True when undoing a kernel transaction brings /boot back with it."""
    r_src, r_root, _ = mount_identity("/")
    b_src, b_root, _ = mount_identity("/boot")
    if not r_src or not b_src:
        return False
    if r_src != b_src:
        return False
    if b_root == r_root:
        return True
    prefix = r_root if r_root.endswith("/") else r_root + "/"
    return b_root.startswith(prefix)


def snapper_configs() -> list:
    """The configuration names snapper knows about, or []."""
    rc, out, _ = run([snapper_bin(), "--csvout", "list-configs",
                      "--columns", "config"], timeout=20)
    if rc != 0:
        return []
    names = []
    for row in csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(out)):
        name = (row.get("config") or "").strip()
        if name:
            names.append(name)
    return names


# ── snapshots ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def list_snapshots(config: str) -> list:
    """Every snapshot in `config` as a dict, read BY HEADER NAME.

    `--columns` names them on the command line and csv.DictReader reads
    them back by name, so this cannot silently shift the day btrfs quota is
    turned on and snapper grows a "Used Space" column — which is exactly
    what a positional `line.split("|")[5]` does.
    """
    rc, out, err = run([snapper_bin(), "--csvout", "-c", config, "list",
                        "--columns", LIST_COLUMNS], timeout=30)
    if rc != 0:
        raise UndoRefused("no-config" if "config" in (err or "").lower()
                          else "no-pair")
    snaps = []
    for row in csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(out)):
        num = (row.get("number") or "").strip()
        if not num.isdigit() or num == "0":
            continue            # row 0 is snapper's "current" pseudo-entry
        snaps.append({
            "number": int(num),
            "type": (row.get("type") or "").strip(),
            "pre_number": (row.get("pre-number") or "").strip(),
            "date": (row.get("date") or "").strip(),
            "description": (row.get("description") or "").strip(),
            "cleanup": (row.get("cleanup") or "").strip(),
            "userdata": (row.get("userdata") or "").strip(),
        })
    return snaps


def find_last_pair(snaps: list) -> tuple:
    """The newest complete pre/post pair, as (pre, post) dicts.

    snap-pac's own record is snapper's TYPE column and its pre-number
    linkage — not a description snap-pac happened to write, which is a
    string that can change between releases. A `post` whose `pre-number`
    names a snapshot that no longer exists is the cleaned-up case and gets
    its own refusal, because "the before half is gone" and "there was never
    a pair" are different facts with different cures.
    """
    by_number = {s["number"]: s for s in snaps}
    posts = sorted((s for s in snaps if s["type"] == "post"),
                   key=lambda s: s["number"], reverse=True)
    if not posts:
        raise UndoRefused("no-pair")
    for post in posts:
        pre_n = post["pre_number"]
        if not pre_n.isdigit():
            continue
        pre = by_number.get(int(pre_n))
        if pre is not None and pre["type"] == "pre":
            return pre, post
    raise UndoRefused("predates")


def status_paths(config: str, pre: int, post: int) -> list:
    """[(code, path)] for every path that differs between the two.

    snapper status prints a six-character status string then the filename;
    column 1 is + created, - deleted, c content, t type. That count IS the
    "how many files would change" the page promises, so it is measured
    here and never estimated from a package count.
    """
    rc, out, err = run([snapper_bin(), "-c", config, "status",
                        "%d..%d" % (pre, post)], timeout=180)
    if rc != 0:
        raise UndoRefused("no-pair")
    rows = []
    for line in (out or "").splitlines():
        if len(line) < 8 or " " not in line:
            continue
        code, _, path = line.partition(" ")
        path = path.strip()
        if not path.startswith("/"):
            continue
        rows.append((code[0], path))
    return rows


# ── the pacman log, for the names a person recognises ────────────────────

def parse_log_time(raw: str):
    """pacman's `2026-08-15T06:43:00-0400` timestamp, or None."""
    raw = (raw or "").strip()
    for fmt in ("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z", "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"):
        try:
            return datetime.strptime(raw, fmt)
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return None


def parse_snapper_time(raw: str):
    """snapper's `2026-08-15 06:43:00` date column, or None."""
    raw = (raw or "").strip()
    for fmt in ("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S", "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"):
        try:
            return datetime.strptime(raw, fmt)
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return None


def packages_between(t0, t1) -> list:
    """[(verb, pkg, versions)] recorded in the pacman log in that window.

    Both bounds are the snapshot timestamps, so this reads exactly the
    transaction the pair bracketed. Timezone-naive on both sides: snapper's
    date column has no offset, pacman's has one, so the pacman side is
    stripped to compare like with like on one machine's own clock.
    """
    out = []
    if t0 is None or t1 is None:
        return out
    try:
        with open(pacman_log_path(), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                m = PACMAN_LINE_RE.match(line)
                if not m:
                    continue
                when = parse_log_time(m.group("when"))
                if when is None:
                    continue
                when = when.replace(tzinfo=None)
                if t0 <= when <= t1:
                    out.append((m.group("verb"), m.group("pkg"),
                                m.group("vers")))
    except OSError:
        return out
    return out


# ── the kernel verdict ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

def kernel_verdict(paths: list, packages: list) -> dict:
    """Is this a kernel/initramfs transaction, and can it be handled?

    The path signal is the authority — it is what actually changed. The
    name signal catches the case where the status read was truncated or a
    DKMS rebuild landed somewhere unexpected. The union is used because
    being wrong in the permissive direction here is how a machine stops
    booting.
    """
    hit_paths = [p for _c, p in paths
                 if p.startswith(KERNEL_PATH_PREFIXES)
                 or p in KERNEL_PATH_EXACT]
    hit_pkgs = [pkg for _v, pkg, _r in packages if KERNEL_PKG_RE.match(pkg)]
    is_kernel = bool(hit_paths) or bool(hit_pkgs)
    verdict = {
        "is_kernel": is_kernel,
        "paths": sorted(set(hit_paths))[:20],
        "packages": sorted(set(hit_pkgs)),
        "boot_inside_snapshot": None,
        "mkinitcpio": None,
        "refusal": None,
    }
    if not is_kernel:
        return verdict
    verdict["boot_inside_snapshot"] = boot_inside_root_snapshot()
    verdict["mkinitcpio"] = bool(shutil.which(mkinitcpio_bin())
                                 or os.path.exists(mkinitcpio_bin()))
    if not verdict["boot_inside_snapshot"]:
        verdict["refusal"] = "kernel-boot-split"
    elif not verdict["mkinitcpio"]:
        verdict["refusal"] = "kernel-no-mkinitcpio"
    return verdict


# ── the plan: one function, so preview and apply cannot disagree ─────────

def build_plan(pair=None) -> dict:
    """Everything the person is shown, and everything apply re-checks.

    `pair` is an explicit (pre, post) snapshot-number pair — the route
    `nyxus-update --rollback` uses (CS-1053 / TRK-1313): the updater takes
    pre-update-/post-update- snapshots around its own transaction and this
    engine, not a second undochange path, reverts between them. Both
    numbers must exist; a missing or misordered pair is its own refusal.
    With pair=None the behaviour is unchanged: the newest snap-pac
    pre/post pair, found by type and pre-number linkage.

    THE PREVIEW AND THE ACTION READ THE SAME FUNCTION. That is deliberate
    and it is the answer to "a person must never click undo and find out
    afterwards what it meant" — there is no second, looser code path that
    could describe the transaction differently from the one that reverts it.
    apply calls this again rather than trusting what the page was shown, so
    a transaction that changed underneath the window is caught.

    Raises UndoRefused for every honest no.
    """
    _, _, root_fs = mount_identity("/")
    if root_fs != "btrfs":
        raise UndoRefused("not-btrfs")
    if not (shutil.which(snapper_bin()) or os.path.exists(snapper_bin())):
        raise UndoRefused("no-snapper")
    configs = snapper_configs()
    if not configs:
        raise UndoRefused("no-config")
    config = "root" if "root" in configs else configs[0]

    snaps = list_snapshots(config)
    if pair is not None:
        by_number = {s["number"]: s for s in snaps}
        pre = by_number.get(pair[0])
        post = by_number.get(pair[1])
        if pre is None or post is None or pair[0] >= pair[1]:
            raise UndoRefused("pair-missing")
    else:
        pre, post = find_last_pair(snaps)
    paths = status_paths(config, pre["number"], post["number"])
    t0 = parse_snapper_time(pre["date"])
    t1 = parse_snapper_time(post["date"])
    packages = packages_between(t0, t1)
    kernel = kernel_verdict(paths, packages)

    counts = {"created": 0, "deleted": 0, "modified": 0, "other": 0}
    for code, _p in paths:
        if code == "+":
            counts["created"] += 1
        elif code == "-":
            counts["deleted"] += 1
        elif code == "c":
            counts["modified"] += 1
        else:
            counts["other"] += 1

    label = post["description"] or pre["description"] or "package transaction"
    return {
        "config": config,
        "pre": pre["number"],
        "post": post["number"],
        "when": pre["date"] or post["date"],
        "label": label,
        "files": len(paths),
        "counts": counts,
        "packages": [{"verb": v, "name": n, "versions": r}
                     for v, n, r in packages],
        "package_count": len(packages),
        "kernel": kernel,
        "sample": [{"code": c, "path": p} for c, p in paths[:40]],
    }


# ── the three privileged steps, each its own function so the ORDER of the
#    calls in cmd_apply is a thing a gate can read ────────────────────────

def _create_pre_undo_snapshot(config: str, pre: int, post: int) -> tuple:
    """(number, error). Taken BEFORE the revert so the undo can be undone.

    `important=yes` puts it under NUMBER_LIMIT_IMPORTANT rather than the
    ordinary NUMBER_LIMIT — both are 10 in the config this build writes
    (nyxus-postinstall step 4b and the Calamares shellprocess twin), so the
    way back from an undo cannot be pruned by ordinary update churn.
    """
    desc = "before undo of #%d..#%d" % (pre, post)
    rc, out, err = run([
        snapper_bin(), "-c", config, "create",
        "--type", "single",
        "--cleanup-algorithm", "number",
        "--userdata", "important=yes,nyxus-undo=safety",
        "--description", desc,
        "--print-number"], timeout=180)
    if rc != 0:
        return (None, (err or out or "snapper create failed").strip())
    num = (out or "").strip().splitlines()
    return ((num[-1].strip() if num else ""), None)


def _run_undochange(config: str, pre: int, post: int) -> tuple:
    """The revert itself. The ONLY call to snapper undochange in this build.

    Gate 13q40 pins that: exactly one caller (`cmd_apply`), unreachable from
    preview or check at any depth, and the word `undochange` appears in no
    other subprocess argv in this file.
    """
    rc, out, err = run([snapper_bin(), "-c", config, "undochange",
                        "%d..%d" % (pre, post)], timeout=1800)
    return (rc, ((out or "") + (err or "")).strip())


def _regenerate_initramfs() -> tuple:
    """mkinitcpio -P, after a kernel undo. Runs LAST, never instead."""
    rc, out, err = run([mkinitcpio_bin(), "-P"], timeout=1800)
    return (rc, ((out or "") + (err or "")).strip())


# ── commands ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def _refusal_payload(key: str) -> dict:
    code, headline, detail = REFUSALS[key]
    return {"ok": False, "refusal": key, "exit": code,
            "headline": headline, "detail": detail}


def _emit(payload: dict, as_json: bool) -> None:
    if as_json:
        json.dump(payload, sys.stdout, indent=2)
        sys.stdout.write("\n")
        return
    if payload.get("refusal"):
        print(payload["headline"])
        print(payload["detail"])
        return
    for line in payload.get("lines", []):
        print(line)


def cmd_check(args) -> int:
    """The cheap capability read. Deliberately touches no snapshot.

    This is what a page may call on every visit: four unprivileged facts and
    no snapper subprocess that needs root, so opening Settings never raises
    an authentication prompt on its own.
    """
    _, _, root_fs = mount_identity("/")
    have_snapper = bool(shutil.which(snapper_bin())
                        or os.path.exists(snapper_bin()))
    try:
        cfg_dir = config_dir_path()
        configured = os.path.isdir(cfg_dir) and bool(os.listdir(cfg_dir))
    except OSError:
        configured = None       # unreadable without privilege — not "no"
    payload = {
        "ok": True,
        "root_fs": root_fs,
        "btrfs": root_fs == "btrfs",
        "snapper": have_snapper,
        "configured": configured,
        "boot_inside_snapshot": boot_inside_root_snapshot(),
        "mkinitcpio": bool(shutil.which(mkinitcpio_bin())),
    }
    if root_fs != "btrfs":
        payload.update(_refusal_payload("not-btrfs"))
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return EXIT_NOT_BTRFS
    if not have_snapper:
        payload.update(_refusal_payload("no-snapper"))
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return EXIT_NO_SNAPPER
    if configured is False:
        payload.update(_refusal_payload("no-config"))
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return EXIT_NO_CONFIG
    payload["lines"] = ["undo is available on this system"]
    _emit(payload, args.json)
    return EXIT_OK


def cmd_preview(args) -> int:
    """Say what would happen. Changes nothing, and cannot: no function it
    reaches, at any depth, creates or reverts a snapshot."""
    try:
        plan = build_plan(getattr(args, "pair", None))
    except UndoRefused as r:
        payload = _refusal_payload(r.key)
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return payload["exit"]

    kernel = plan["kernel"]
    if kernel["refusal"]:
        payload = _refusal_payload(kernel["refusal"])
        payload["plan"] = plan
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return payload["exit"]

    c = plan["counts"]
    lines = [
        "%s" % plan["label"],
        "ran %s · snapshots #%d → #%d" % (plan["when"], plan["pre"],
                                          plan["post"]),
        "%d package%s changed" % (plan["package_count"],
                                  "" if plan["package_count"] == 1 else "s"),
        "%d file%s would change back "
        "(%d restored, %d removed, %d re-created)"
        % (plan["files"], "" if plan["files"] == 1 else "s",
           c["modified"], c["created"], c["deleted"]),
    ]
    if kernel["is_kernel"]:
        lines.append("this is a kernel update — the startup image will be "
                     "rebuilt after the undo")
    payload = {"ok": True, "refusal": None, "plan": plan, "lines": lines}
    _emit(payload, args.json)
    return EXIT_OK


def cmd_apply(args) -> int:
    """Undo it — in the one order that is safe, and no other.

    ★ THE ORDER IS THE SAFETY ARGUMENT, and gate 13q40 reads it as line
      numbers rather than as a comment:

        consent → plan → kernel guard → PRE-UNDO SNAPSHOT → revert → initramfs

      Nothing may be inserted ahead of the consent return, and the revert
      may not be reached until the snapshot that undoes it exists.
    """
    # 1 · CONSENT. Before any read, before any subprocess.
    if not args.confirm:
        payload = _refusal_payload("no-consent")
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return EXIT_NO_CONSENT

    # 2 · the plan, rebuilt from the system as it is NOW — never the one
    #     the page was holding, which may be minutes old.
    try:
        plan = build_plan(getattr(args, "pair", None))
    except UndoRefused as r:
        payload = _refusal_payload(r.key)
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return payload["exit"]

    # 3 · THE KERNEL GUARD, ahead of the action and not beside it.
    kernel = plan["kernel"]
    if kernel["refusal"]:
        payload = _refusal_payload(kernel["refusal"])
        payload["plan"] = plan
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return payload["exit"]

    # 4 · THE PRE-UNDO SNAPSHOT. Undo is a change; this is the way back
    #     from it. If it fails, NOTHING is reverted — a revert with no way
    #     back is worse than the broken update the person came here with.
    safety, snap_err = _create_pre_undo_snapshot(
        plan["config"], plan["pre"], plan["post"])
    if snap_err is not None:
        payload = {
            "ok": False, "refusal": "snapshot-failed",
            "exit": EXIT_SNAPSHOT_FAILED,
            "headline": "Nothing was undone — the safety snapshot failed.",
            "detail": "An undo is itself a change, so a restore point is "
                      "taken first and the undo does not run without one. "
                      "snapper said: " + snap_err,
            "plan": plan}
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return EXIT_SNAPSHOT_FAILED

    # 5 · only now, the revert.
    rc, undo_out = _run_undochange(plan["config"], plan["pre"], plan["post"])
    if rc != 0:
        payload = {
            "ok": False, "refusal": "undo-failed",
            "exit": EXIT_UNDO_FAILED,
            "headline": "The undo did not complete.",
            "detail": "snapper undochange exited %d: %s. Restore point #%s "
                      "was taken before it started." % (rc, undo_out, safety),
            "safety_snapshot": safety, "plan": plan}
        _emit(payload, args.json)
        return EXIT_UNDO_FAILED

    # 6 · the initramfs, if this was a kernel transaction. Last, and only
    #     ever in addition to the revert.
    initramfs = None
    if kernel["is_kernel"]:
        irc, iout = _regenerate_initramfs()
        initramfs = {"rc": irc, "output": iout[-4000:]}
        if irc != 0:
            payload = {
                "ok": False, "refusal": "initramfs-failed",
                "exit": EXIT_INITRAMFS_FAILED,
                "headline": "The update was undone, but the startup image "
                            "could not be rebuilt.",
                "detail": "DO NOT REBOOT until this is resolved. mkinitcpio "
                          "exited %d: %s. Restore point #%s holds the state "
                          "from before the undo." % (irc, iout[-400:], safety),
                "safety_snapshot": safety, "initramfs": initramfs,
                "plan": plan}
            _emit(payload, args.json)
            return EXIT_INITRAMFS_FAILED

    payload = {
        "ok": True, "refusal": None,
        "headline": "Undone.",
        "detail": "%s was reverted. Restore point #%s holds the state from "
                  "before the undo, so this can itself be undone."
                  % (plan["label"], safety),
        "safety_snapshot": safety,
        "undo_output": undo_out,
        "initramfs": initramfs,
        "plan": plan,
        "lines": ["Undone: " + plan["label"],
                  undo_out,
                  "restore point #%s holds the state from before the undo"
                  % safety],
    }
    if kernel["is_kernel"]:
        payload["lines"].append("startup image rebuilt (mkinitcpio -P)")
    _emit(payload, args.json)
    return EXIT_OK


def _pair_arg(text: str):
    """PRE..POST as two snapshot numbers — the shape snapper itself uses."""
    m = re.match(r"^(\d+)\.\.(\d+)$", text)
    if not m:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(
            "expected PRE..POST (two snapshot numbers, e.g. 41..42)")
    return (int(m.group(1)), int(m.group(2)))


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="nyxus-undo",
        description="Undo the last package transaction — not a rollback.")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true",
                    help="machine-readable output for the Settings page")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd")

    p_check = sub.add_parser("check", help="cheap capability report")
    p_check.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p_check.set_defaults(func=cmd_check)

    p_prev = sub.add_parser("preview", help="what undoing would change")
    p_prev.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p_prev.add_argument("--pair", type=_pair_arg, metavar="PRE..POST",
                        help="an explicit snapshot pair instead of the "
                             "newest snap-pac one (nyxus-update --rollback)")
    p_prev.set_defaults(func=cmd_preview)

    p_app = sub.add_parser("apply", help="undo it (requires --confirm)")
    p_app.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p_app.add_argument("--confirm", action="store_true",
                       help="the person asked for this in as many words")
    p_app.add_argument("--pair", type=_pair_arg, metavar="PRE..POST",
                       help="an explicit snapshot pair instead of the "
                            "newest snap-pac one (nyxus-update --rollback)")
    p_app.set_defaults(func=cmd_apply)

    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if not getattr(args, "cmd", None):
        ap.print_help()
        return EXIT_USAGE
    return args.func(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
